  "preamble": "Fronting of the mid back rounded vowel [o(ː)] to the front rounded [ø(ː)], with lip rounding kept. It is the Germanic i-umlaut of *o and *ō: an /i/ or /j/ in the next syllable pulled the back vowel forward. Old Norse ǿ (also written œ), Anglian Old English œ̄ (written oe) and Middle High German œ all stand for [øː]. Short *o was rare before i or j in Germanic, so almost all examples involve the long vowel. In German the umlaut vowel probably existed as an allophone in Old High German but was first written in Middle High German, where it became a phoneme.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Fronting, Umlaut, I-Mutation",
